#
# tests/insn/x86_64.insn
# ======================
#
# The x86-64 instructions whose EasyCrypt models the proofs of the SHA-NI
# kernel (src/x86_64/shani) rest on, in the form tests/insn/insn.py reads:
# a name, one Jasmin statement over the operands a to h and the results
# r to u, and the mnemonic it must compile to. Operands appear in the
# order of the Jasmin intrinsic, which is the order of the arguments of
# the EasyCrypt operator (JModel_x86.ec).
#
# SHA256RNDS2 takes its third operand in XMM0; the register allocator
# places c there.

sha256rnds2  r = #SHA256RNDS2(a, b, c);  sha256rnds2
sha256msg1   r = #SHA256MSG1(a, b);      sha256msg1
sha256msg2   r = #SHA256MSG2(a, b);      sha256msg2
